:       * cite26†Google Workspace L151:       * cite27†Learning & Education L152:       * cite28†Shopping L153:       * cite29†See all L154:     * Platforms\nL155: \nL156:       * cite30†Android L157:       * cite31†Google Play L158:       * cite32†Wear OS L159:       * cite33†See all L160:     * Devices\nL161:       * cite34†Pixel L162:       * cite35†Google Nest L163:       * cite36†Fitbit L164:       * cite37†Chromebook L165:       * cite38†Googlebook L166:       * cite39†See all L167: \nL168: Learn more:\nL169: \nL170: cite40†Google Ads & Commerce blog cite41†Waze blog L171: \nL172:   * Company news\nL173: ### Company news\nL174: \nL175: See all in Company news\nL176: \nL177:     * Outreach & initiatives\nL178: \nL179:       * cite42†Creating opportunity L180:       * cite15†Safety & security L181:       * cite43†Google.org L182:       * cite44†Public policy L183:       * cite45†Sustainability L184:       * cite16†Health L185:       * cite46†See all L186:     * Leadership\nL187: \nL188:       * cite47†Sundar Pichai, CEO L189:       * cite48†More authors L190:       * cite48†See all L191:     * Inside Google\nL192: \nL193:       * cite49†Around the globe L194:       * cite50†Life at Google L195:       * cite51†See all L196: \nL197: Learn more:\nL198: \nL199: cite52†Google Security blog L200: \nL201:   * cite1†Feed L202: Running Guide agent: A step towards running unbounded\nL203: \nL204: []\nL205: \nL206: Preferences\nL207: \nL208:   * [Select]\nL209: \nL210: Links\nL211: \nL212:   * cite53†Images L213:   * cite54†RSS feed L214: # Running Guide agent: A step towards running unbounded\nL215: \nL216: May 20, 2026\nL217: \nL218: |\nL219: \nL220: Running Guide Agent is a running assistant that helps blind and low-vision athletes run independently without physical lines or human guides.\nL221: \nL222: * * *\nL223: \nL224: Robin Dua\nL225: \nL226: Senior Director, AI Innovation & Research, Platforms & Devices\nL227: \nL228: Miguel de Andrés-Clavera\nL229: \nL230: Group Product Manager, Google DeepMind\nL231: \nL232: * * *\nL233: \nL234: Your browser does not support the audio element.\nL235: \nL236: Listen to article\nL237: \nL238: [[duration]] minutes\nL239: This content is generated by Google AI. Generative AI is experimental\nL240: \nL241: [Input]\nL242: \nL243: Voice Speed\nL244: \nL245: Voice\nL246: \nL247: Speed 0.75X 1X 1.5X 2X\nL248: For blind and low-vision (BLV) athletes, running has traditionally required a physical tether — whether it’s a human guide or a painted track line. Today, we are excited to share how we’re taking steps towards changing that with the Running Guide agent, an accessibility agent that uses real-time environmental understanding to help low-vision athletes run. It marks a massive leap from simple path-following to advanced, real-time spatial reasoning.\nL249: As we work to perfect this technology, our goal is simple: unassisted independence for every runner.\nL250: ## A hybrid architecture for uncompromising safety\nL251: \nL252: Building on our previous work with cite55†Project Guideline†youtu.be , the Running Guide agent uses a chest-mounted Pixel 10 Pro smartphone to view the path ahead and guide the user via auditory feedback. Because high-speed activities demand high trust, we built a hybrid, dual-path architecture:\nL253:   * On-device segmentation: Running entirely offline on the Pixel 10’s custom silicon, this model guarantees ultra-low latency safety. It delivers immediate \"STOP\" alerts and steering cues — heard as directional ticking sounds — so runners maintain a reliable sense of direction even without a cellular connection.\nL254:   * Gemma 4’s advanced reasoning: Leveraging cite56†Gemma 4 E4B†huggingface.co , this path handles complex multimodal inputs (image and text) for high-level scene understanding entirely on device. To keep latency low, we use Smarter Frame Selection. Instead of processing every frame, the model only analyzes \"high-entropy\" frames — like sudden terrain changes or new obstacles — delivering faster, highly relevant coaching.\nL255: cite57†Image: Running guide agent architecture diagram. Flowchart maps Input Senses (video, audio, Pixel 10 Pro) to On-Device Analysis (Segmentation) and Agents (Planner, Coach, Break), leading to Output Feedback (Partial Audio and Voice Coaching)†storage.googleapis.com L256: ## A multi-agent framework\nL257: \nL258: The Running Guide agent is a collaborative, multi-agent framework designed to help enable the running experience for a BLV user:\nL259:   * Planner agent: Utilizing Gemma 4’s function calling, this agent pulls weather and Google Maps data, chats with the runner to establish workout goals and calibrates their digital starting line.\nL260:   * Coach agent: Operates mid-run to deliver concise, telegraphic verbal alerts. It triages feedback into a strict hierarchy: DANGER (immediate evasive action needed), WARNING (nearby runners/obstacles) and NOTICE (upcoming track curves).\nL261:   * Break agent: Manages rest intervals, allowing athletes to pause and resume their session at any time.\nL262: ## Intelligent eyewear and community partnerships\nL263: \nL264: While the chest-mounted Pixel 10 Pro is a robust foundation, we are prototyping the Running Guide agent on intelligent eyewear. Wearable glasses provide a wider, steadier field of view, which drastically optimizes the data fed to our multimodal models. The glasses stream directly to the Pixel device, seamlessly blending hardware and ambient AI.\nL265: \nL266: Runner’s view through Intelligent Eyewear\nL267: To ensure we are building alongside the community, we’ve partnered with cite58†SG Enable†www.sgenable.sg , Singapore’s focal agency for disability and inclusion. By connecting our engineering teams directly with BLV runners for real-world testing, we can iteratively design a tool that truly meets their needs.\nL268: The Running Guide agent is a powerful showcase of the new chapter of agents for Google AI. Athletes will be able to use our tool, which combines zero-latency edge computing with deep-world understanding, to push their limits and navigate the world with total, unassisted confidence.\nL269: ## Get the latest news from Google in your inbox\nL270: \nL271: Sign up for our newsletters with product updates, event information, special offers, and more.\nL272: \nL273: Done. Just one step more.\nL274: \nL275: Check your inbox to confirm your subscription.\nL276: \nL277: You can also subscribe with a [Button: different email address].\nL278: \nL279: Your information will be used in accordance with cite59†Google's privacy policy.†policies.google.com You may opt out at any time.\nL280: \nL281: Posted in:\nL282: \nL283:   * cite2†Google DeepMind L284:   * cite60†Accessibility L285:   * cite61†Google in Asia L286:   * Privacy\nL287:   * Terms\nL288:   * Help\nL289:   * [Select]\nL290: \nL291:   * [Select]\n"
